" ~/.vimrc — minimal, Gruvbox dark, zero plugins.
"
" Deliberately narrow: tabs, the same colour scheme as the rest of this
" project, search, a file tree for navigating a project the way an editor
" with a sidebar does, closing a file without closing vim, and copying to
" and from the system clipboard. Nothing here traces to anything else, on
" purpose — this file is optional and lives apart from every host's own
" install.sh. README.md's "Optional: vim" section is the place for how to
" actually use any of this — search and replace, block-visual multi-line
" editing, copy/paste — most of which needs no mapping at all, only knowing
" it is there.

set nocompatible
filetype plugin indent on
syntax on
set encoding=utf-8

" Leader — Ctrl-v, not the default backslash, and not any of the handful of
" keys this project's tmux.conf claims for itself. Has to be set before any
" mapping below uses <leader>, since the substitution happens once, at the
" point each mapping is defined.
"
" Ctrl-v is what vim itself uses, in Normal mode with nothing set, to enter
" Visual Block, and that is barely affected: vim only defers to a leader
" mapping when the very next key completes one (here, e or f); anything
" else — j, a digit, whatever a real block selection actually starts with —
" falls straight through to Visual Block immediately, because vim can
" already see that next key queued up and does not need to wait it out.
" Verified with feedkeys(), not assumed: "<C-v>jjy" on three lines produced
" a genuine blockwise yank (getregtype() came back "\x161", vim's own
" blockwise marker) with these mappings active. The actual, narrow cost is
" pressing Ctrl-v and then literally e or f as the first motion of a block
" selection — Ctrl-q sidesteps even that, with no ambiguity to resolve.
" Chosen because vim ships no Normal-mode meaning for it at all (see
" :help i_CTRL-Q — the "same as Ctrl-v" note there is Insert and
" command-line mode only, a literal-next-character insert, unrelated to
" Visual Block), and tmux does not claim it either, stock or in this
" project's tmux.conf. Visual mode's own Ctrl-v — switching an already-open
" selection to blockwise — is untouched either way, since only Normal-mode
" mappings are changed here.
let mapleader = "\<C-v>"
nnoremap <C-q> <C-v>

" Colour scheme — vendored at colors/gruvbox.vim, not fetched. Same hex
" values as the tmux status bar and the bash prompt elsewhere in this
" project, so a terminal running all three reads as one system.
set background=dark
silent! colorscheme gruvbox

" Tabs — spaces, not literal tab characters, so width holds regardless of the
" editor or terminal reading the file afterwards. 4 is a general default; a
" project with its own convention should override this per-filetype rather
" than change it here.
set tabstop=4
set shiftwidth=4
set softtabstop=4
set expandtab
set autoindent
set smartindent

" The tab line (buffer/window tabs across the top) stays visible with even
" one tab open, the way an editor with a permanent tab bar would.
set showtabline=2

" Switching tabs needs no mapping at all: gt and gT are vim's own defaults,
" already unclaimed by tmux since they are plain letters and tmux only ever
" claims specific chords and function keys, never ordinary keystrokes. An
" earlier version of this file bound Shift-Left/Shift-Right instead, which
" this project's tmux.conf binds first, at its root key table with no
" prefix needed — meaning tmux, not vim, always saw those two keys, and the
" mapping never once fired inside a tmux pane. gt/gT sidestep the whole
" problem rather than finding a key tmux happens not to want yet.

" Search — highlighted and incremental as you type; case-insensitive unless
" the pattern itself contains a capital, which is smartcase's whole point.
" Ctrl-l already redraws the screen; clearing stale highlighting rides on the
" same key rather than claiming a new one, since a redraw is usually wanted
" for the same reason the highlighting is stale.
set incsearch
set hlsearch
set ignorecase
set smartcase
nnoremap <silent> <C-l> :nohlsearch<CR><C-l>

" Project-wide search through the quickfix list. Uses ripgrep when it is on
" the machine; falls back to vim's own (slow) internal grep otherwise rather
" than erroring, since a build without rg should still be able to search.
if executable('rg')
    set grepprg=rg\ --vimgrep\ --no-heading\ --smart-case
    set grepformat=%f:%l:%c:%m
endif
command! -nargs=+ -complete=file Search silent! grep! <args> | copen
nnoremap <leader>f :Search<Space>

" File tree — leader e (mnemonic: Explorer) toggles a left-hand explorer,
" using netrw, which ships with vim already. :Lexplore is netrw's own
" toggle: called again while the tree is open, it closes it, so no custom
" logic is needed here.
"
" Not bound to bare Ctrl-b, which an earlier version of this file used:
" Ctrl-b is this project's tmux prefix, unconditionally, on every host —
" tmux consumes it before vim, or anything else running in the pane, ever
" sees it. A mapping on the prefix key itself cannot work inside tmux, which
" is where vim runs here essentially all the time.
let g:netrw_banner = 0
let g:netrw_liststyle = 3
let g:netrw_winsize = 25
let g:netrw_browse_split = 4
let g:netrw_altv = 1
nnoremap <silent> <leader>e :Lexplore<CR>

" Closing the file you are looking at is not the same thing as closing vim,
" and :q/:x conflate them the moment only one window is open — which it
" usually is. :q closes the current *window*; with nothing else open, vim
" has nothing left to show and exits, which is correct, not a bug, but reads
" as "vim just quit on me" to anyone who meant "I am done with this file."
" Bd instead closes the *buffer*: with another one still open it switches to
" it, and with none left it leaves an empty buffer and keeps running either
" way. Verified both cases directly — two files open, Bd on one lands on the
" other; Bd again, on the last one, still leaves vim running.
function! CloseBuffer() abort
    let l:buf = bufnr('%')
    if len(getbufinfo({'buflisted': 1})) > 1
        bnext
    endif
    execute 'bdelete' l:buf
endfunction
command! Bd call CloseBuffer()
nnoremap <silent> <leader>q :Bd<CR>

" Copy and paste to the system clipboard, not just vim's own registers — so
" text copied here can be pasted into another tmux pane, and the reverse.
" This vim package ships without +clipboard (confirmed with vim --version;
" true on every host here, not just this one), so the built-in "+ register
" does nothing. Piped through xclip instead, the same tool this project's
" own `cpy` bash function already uses, and guarded the same way: no
" display, no mapping, silently — correct on the headless hosts, where
" there is nothing to pipe to.
"
" leader-y in Visual mode: pressing : from an active selection has vim
" prepend '<,'> automatically, scoping the write to exactly what is
" selected, not the whole file. leader-p in Normal mode reads the clipboard
" into the buffer below the cursor. Both verified with a real xclip round
" trip, not assumed: yanked two of three lines, only those two arrived;
" pasted a marker, it landed on exactly the expected line.
if executable('xclip') && !empty($DISPLAY)
    vnoremap <leader>y :w !xclip -selection clipboard<CR><CR>
    nnoremap <leader>p :r !xclip -selection clipboard -o<CR>
endif

" Clicking a file in that tree, or anywhere else, should do what it looks
" like it does.
set mouse=a

" Line numbers, sane split directions, and a fixed backspace — vim's own
" default (indent,eol,start unset) refuses to delete past where insert mode
" began, which reads as broken to anyone arriving from another editor.
set number
set nowrap
set splitright
set splitbelow
set backspace=indent,eol,start
